## 5. Null Safety & Immutability

### 5.1 Model Optionality Explicitly — Avoid Nullable "Just in Case"
A nullable field should mean "this value can genuinely be absent," not "I didn't want
to think about initialization."

```dart
// ❌ BAD: nullable fields that are never actually supposed to be null
class User {
  String? name;
  String? email;
}
final user = User();
print(user.name!.toUpperCase()); // ticking time bomb: `!` on an uninitialized field

// ✅ GOOD: required, non-nullable by default; nullable only for genuinely optional data
class User {
  final String name;
  final String email;
  final String? middleName; // genuinely optional
  const User({required this.name, required this.email, this.middleName});
}
```

### 5.2 Prefer `final`/`const` — Make Immutability the Default
Mutable shared state is one of the most common sources of hard-to-reproduce bugs.
Default to immutable; opt in to mutability only when you have a real reason.

```dart
// ❌ BAD: mutable fields invite accidental external mutation
class Point {
  double x;
  double y;
  Point(this.x, this.y);
}
final p = Point(1, 2);
someFunction(p); // could silently mutate p.x / p.y inside

// ✅ GOOD: immutable value object — safe to pass around, easy to reason about
final class Point {
  final double x;
  final double y;
  const Point(this.x, this.y);

  Point copyWith({double? x, double? y}) => Point(x ?? this.x, y ?? this.y);
}
const origin = Point(0, 0);
```

### 5.3 Use `?.`, `??`, and `??=` Instead of Manual Null Checks
Null-aware operators are shorter and remove a whole class of `if (x != null)` noise.

```dart
// ❌ BAD
String getDisplayName(User? user) {
  if (user != null && user.nickname != null) {
    return user.nickname!;
  }
  return 'Guest';
}

// ✅ GOOD
String getDisplayName(User? user) => user?.nickname ?? 'Guest';
```

